spaces), gluing two solid tori along their boundaries produces $S^3$ if and onlyif the meridian of one is identified with a curve of slope $(1, q)$ on the
other, giving $L(1, q) \cong S^3$. By the uniqueness of genus-1 Heegaard
splittings of $S^3$ up to isotopy (any Heegaard torus in $S^3$ is isotopic to
the standard one), both $A$ and $B$ are unknotted solid tori in $S^3$.

